-- title:
--   Nonvanishing of the ideals (X-x, Y-y) in R[W]
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a nontrivial commutative ring and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $R$ in affine form, with affine coordinate ring $R[W] = R[X][Y]/(Y^2 + a_1XY + a_3Y - (X^3 + a_2X^2 + a_4X + a_6))$. For any element $x \in R$ and any univariate polynomial $y \in R[X]$, the ideal `XYIdeal W x y` of $R[W]$ — the ideal generated by the class of $X - x$ and the class of $Y - y(X)$, i.e. by `XClass W x` and `YClass W y` — is not the zero ideal. No hypothesis is imposed relating $x$ and $y$ to the curve: the pair need not be a point of $W$, and indeed $y$ is allowed to be an arbitrary polynomial rather than a constant, so the conclusion is the purely algebraic assertion that these two generators do not both vanish in $R[W]$.
--
--   For an affine point $P = (x, y)$ of $W$ this is the statement that the point ideal $\mathfrak m_P \subset R[W]$ is nonzero; combined with its maximality it is what places $\mathfrak m_P$ in the height-one spectrum of the coordinate ring, that is, exhibits $P$ as a finite place of the function field of the curve. It is used throughout the development of places and divisors on genus-one curves in this formalisation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_Affine_CoordinateRing_XYIdeal_ne_bot.lean

